WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.520
Network hardening is the process of reducing unnecessary risk in network design,

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.020
configuration, access, and operations.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.960
It does not mean making the network impossible to use.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:16.380
A hardened network still supports intended communication: users reach approved services,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.080
applications reach required dependencies, administrators can maintain systems,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.700
and monitoring can observe the environment.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.640
The difference is that unnecessary exposure is removed, risky defaults are corrected,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.500
and access is controlled deliberately.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.600
The practical themes are consistent across small offices, enterprise campuses,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.300
data centers, and cloud networks.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.440
You need an asset inventory, owners for each subnet and service,

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.940
segmentation that reflects business function and sensitivity,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.440
least privilege for firewall rules and access control lists,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:55.220
secure remote administration, reliable patch and firmware processes,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.680
and logs that help teams see what changed.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.860
None of those controls works well when treated as a one-time cleanup.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.200
A useful hardening program also protects availability.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:11.380
Changes to a firewall rule, VLAN, VPN, DNS service, DHCP scope, wireless network,

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.960
or management interface can disrupt production if they are rushed.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.820
Defensive work should use approval, maintenance windows, configuration backups, testing,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.720
and rollback plans.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.700
Good hardening removes what is not needed, protects what must remain,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.800
and preserves the ability to operate the network safely.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.420
The core idea is lifecycle thinking.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.520
Inventory changes as assets appear and retire.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.540
Baselines change as vendors release updates.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.320
Firewall rules drift as projects come and go.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:44.780
Exceptions may be necessary, but they should be documented and reviewed.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.640
Network hardening is disciplined risk reduction through design, configuration, monitoring,

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.820
validation, and continuous improvement.
